{"skill":{"slug":"computer-use","displayName":"Computer Use"},"version":{"version":"1.2.1","createdAt":1771195222594,"changelog":"- Improved description to highlight X11-level automation for undetectable interactions (vs browser tools).\n- Clarified Chrome usage: added instructions to check user namespace support and avoid unnecessary --no-sandbox flag (improves browser stability).\n- Updated manual setup instructions to suggest using the setup script for generating service files.\n- Various documentation refinements for clarity and accuracy.","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":6218,"sha256":"6c5447dacdfa36a87ceed5734e75268dce6dd81046c0d14fa7150fc5506bc7d2","contentType":null},{"path":"scripts/click.sh","size":882,"sha256":"ac3163e285c64e0333d51870ae6048a6a9855063e99340e5e4338644edbfecee","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/cursor_position.sh","size":178,"sha256":"6304f9b73f71e8e0cbd11409d8d461c76aef95fa30b11316e1a035609b925660","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/drag.sh","size":473,"sha256":"ed190dcd20d4efdb7f30074715d1fa57443b520cf2c516aff6374c1613383795","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/hold_key.sh","size":612,"sha256":"c8f0b62df7a5137ec23fe89610893fde4ba63cc04016e39027da7a414716fad2","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/key.sh","size":358,"sha256":"7e86171134c4a6e53f0b28c4c138bee308fdb11a67cbb2e8ae648cbef0f3ed97","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/minimal-desktop.sh","size":1253,"sha256":"47c3768c2090aa9ea4b6374d40edd5a64ee3b001462f4681991746a3a007209a","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/mouse_down.sh","size":136,"sha256":"4345aa680bd219e12a6ce287518a15212cbffbe6a16aad8109b57f248620cb1c","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/mouse_move.sh","size":276,"sha256":"ed514a7a078cbaec77fd75db5341ad0e5e3d43d7d73b20e16d463e49a7cd6784","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/mouse_up.sh","size":199,"sha256":"dc4ebb94eb7942cc607ac08f8480530ba506de621f9014140f1586220861575e","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/screenshot.sh","size":438,"sha256":"795b01c5bfb558ad3c52242b4d49a252a0b583a1dd17bc0a73024cfe448b3f1f","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/scroll.sh","size":924,"sha256":"d2b048c5e86c9c8b872bcde73e84e6483d3cc3c5d0a0be89a28f96dc6e258415","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/setup-vnc.sh","size":3712,"sha256":"232abfc9075dcd1ae50965120e7bc42d6a72fc3e55be0be62e868eb84f59154a","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/type_text.sh","size":634,"sha256":"ed043734d1852a3e7ed9bfccef3a7ea1347bba40a6b12ec67731a906e83259e9","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/vnc_start.sh","size":891,"sha256":"8aefde5b1c2fed1e75b4857633a2f1dd459c56be48d4d3dcb733f0e9ac538ca0","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/vnc_stop.sh","size":365,"sha256":"cd41f1a30e1418d97ea2779d4982f8a4a50477f353f286a44303a1bef2c565ca","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/wait.sh","size":488,"sha256":"78ad5f76a13cbf388ebc1e4d231a76aaa26310e4b61ad1972802dbafe70e6019","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"scripts/zoom.sh","size":999,"sha256":"c0b04a123cdc8d69960807d40579a78e69644b982fa9806c22e9647dfeeae6f2","contentType":"text/x-sh"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2014,"sha256":"da24d910d11129290687ed258dbf45b099a4b8fdc1ce898b51e620361879f87b","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789026458718,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"813c0b1fdf3d87eccb80ffaecef13ce9b54d7a07dc8d727faa7de7c08b3f22f9","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/813c0b1fdf3d87eccb80ffaecef13ce9b54d7a07dc8d727faa7de7c08b3f22f9","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: computer-use\nVersion: 1.2.1\n\nThe skill bundle is designed for legitimate desktop control, but contains several security vulnerabilities. The most critical is `scripts/vnc_start.sh`, which starts an `x11vnc` server with the `-nopw` flag, allowing unauthenticated local access to the virtual desktop. While the recommended `setup-vnc.sh` script does not use this flag for its service, the presence of `vnc_start.sh` in the bundle introduces a significant risk if invoked. Additionally, `scripts/cursor_position.sh` uses `eval`, and scripts like `scripts/key.sh` and `scripts/hold_key.sh` pass user input directly to `xdotool`, which could be exploited via prompt injection or if `xdotool` has parsing vulnerabilities, allowing for potentially disruptive key sequences or commands.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779160657456},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":39,"checkedAt":1789026458718},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This skill does what it says, but it installs persistent remote desktop control with weak access controls and makes system-wide desktop changes that users should review carefully.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Persistent VNC/noVNC remote desktop exposure and global xfdesktop replacement are broader than needed for on-demand computer-use automation, even though remote viewing is part of the skill's purpose.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The setup script uses sudo to install packages, write system-wide systemd unit files, copy files under /opt, enable boot services, and replace /usr/bin/xfdesktop with a no-op wrapper.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill gives broad GUI control primitives that can click, type, press keys, capture screenshots, and control any application on display :99, with limited guidance about high-risk actions or sensitive on-screen data.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The setup enables xvfb, xfce-minimal, x11vnc, and novnc at boot with Restart=always; VNC is started without password authentication in standalone mode, and the persistent service lacks an explicit localhost bind or authentication setting.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The stated purpose is headless Linux desktop automation, and the shipped scripts for Xvfb, XFCE, xdotool actions, screenshots, and VNC/noVNC are broadly aligned with that purpose.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"note"}],"guidance":"Install only on an isolated server or VM you are comfortable dedicating to remote GUI automation. Before running setup-vnc.sh, review the systemd services, require localhost-only binding and authentication for VNC/noVNC, and be prepared to remove the services and restore /usr/bin/xfdesktop. Do not expose ports 5900 or 6080 publicly.","findings":"[T06] unexpected: Confirmed: setup-vnc.sh creates and enables boot-persistent system services with automatic restart. Persistent availability is disclosed, but it is a high-impact default for a remote desktop control skill.\n[T09] unexpected: Confirmed: VNC/noVNC setup does not enforce strong authentication and the persistent x11vnc service does not explicitly bind to loopback, making access control dependent on defaults and network configuration.\n[T07] unexpected: Confirmed: setup-vnc.sh renames /usr/bin/xfdesktop and installs a replacement wrapper. The wrapper only exits, so this is not evidence of malicious payload execution, but it is unnecessary system-wide tool interception.\n[PE2] expected: Confirmed sudo use for package installation and systemd setup. Elevated access is partly expected for system service installation, but the scope of changes is broad.\n[SQP-2] unexpected: Confirmed weakly scoped remote desktop exposure and automatic screenshots after many actions. Screenshots are disclosed in the skill tips, but privacy and access-control warnings are not strong enough for the capability.\n[SDI-1] expected: Confirmed several action scripts automatically call screenshot.sh after the action. This is disclosed in SKILL.md as a general behavior, so it is purpose-aligned observability rather than hidden exfiltration.\n[SDI-4] unexpected: Partly confirmed: some script comments describe only the input action while the implementation also captures a screenshot. The top-level skill documentation does disclose that most actions auto-screenshot, reducing but not removing the mismatch.\n[TM2] expected: The reported pipe-to-sudo patterns are ordinary shell file-generation mechanics in setup-vnc.sh. The material issue is not chaining itself, but the privileged system files and executable replacement being written.\n[LP3] unexpected: Confirmed: the skill relies on shell scripts with package installation, service management, screenshots, and GUI automation, but does not define an explicit permissions boundary or least-privilege operating mode.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789026458718}}}}}